 "action_summary": "The owner asked, for the fifth time, why the machine cannot simply be shown a coding action once and then have it. The owner also said the solution already exists under a non-obvious name. It does: canonical/swe_kernel/causal_trace_to_skill_compiler_v1.py ('Watch/Explore Once -> Permanent Skill') compiles one demonstration into a parameterized skill, demands a second distinct instance as proof, and the front door falls back to it when no row serves a request. So the owner's experiment was run through the front door on main. A file task taught once was then done on a new file (PASS__COMPILED_WORKFLOW_SKILL), and four trap sentences were refused. A coding action (rename a routine) gave two defects. Shown as the file after the edit, it compiled into 'replace the named file with the taught file', passed qualification because its postcondition was true of the taught file too, and at the front door replaced another file and answered PASS. Shown as a program, it did the right thing on a new file, but on the host: solve_v1.act imports the sandbox by a top-level name that resolves only when canonical/runtime is on sys.path, the except clause swallowed the error, and the receipt said confined False. This change adds one guard (a generalized write_text may create a file, fill an empty one, repeat itself, or update a file the same step wrote; it may not replace anything else), asks it both where a skill is qualified and where it is recalled, runs a taught program inside the existing namespace sandbox or refuses it before any earlier step writes, and lets act find the sandbox from a package import. No row is added, no route is opened, nothing that was refused becomes allowed.",
